 <h2 id="chandas"><code>chandas</code></h2>
 <p class="mod-desc">After Pingala&#x27;s Chandahśāstra (~2nd c. BCE). Sanskrit metre is built from two syllable weights — laghu (light) and guru (heavy) — so an n-syllable metre is a sequence of two choices: there are exactly 2^n of them. Pingala gave the doubling rule for 2^n, the Meru-prastāra (Pascal&#x27;s triangle) to count how many metres have exactly k guru syllables, and the mātrā-meru, which generates the Fibonacci numbers. A pattern is carried as an integer bitmask: bit i (LSB = first syllable) is 1 for guru, 0 for laghu. The integer value IS the pattern&#x27;s index in the prastāra.</p>
