 <p class="mod-desc">After Aryabhata&#x27;s Kuttaka (~499 CE) and the Chakravala of Jayadeva and Bhaskara II (~1150 CE). KUTTAKA (&quot;the pulveriser&quot;) solves the linear indeterminate equation ax + by = c. It is the ancestor of the extended Euclidean algorithm. CHAKRAVALA (&quot;the wheel&quot;) solves the quadratic indeterminate equation x^2 - N*y^2 = 1, known in Europe as Pell&#x27;s equation. It was the first complete method for it, and for N = 61 it gives x = 1766319049, y = 226153980 - a case Europe first solved in 1657. kuttaka(a, b, c) -&gt; x such that a*x is congruent to c (mod b). Requires gcd(a, b) to divide c. Returns x (a particular solution).</p>
